{"cartConfigs":{"isActive":false},"dynamic_pages":[],"options":{"mobileOnly":false,"versionID":null},"page":[{"id":"sec_2101","type":"section","responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"background":"rgba(255,255,255,1)","height":72}},"mobile":{"styles":{"background":"rgba(255,255,255,1)","height":66}}},"children":[{"id":"t_2102","type":"text-block","specials":{"text":"<a href=\"https://www.dijaedadula.com/home\" style=\"color:rgba(26,26,26,1);text-decoration:none\">DIJAE DADULA</a>"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":16,"fontWeight":"800","height":24,"left":160,"letterSpacing":"3px","lineHeight":"1.4","top":26,"width":260}},"mobile":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":14,"fontWeight":"800","height":22,"left":20,"letterSpacing":"2px","lineHeight":"1.4","top":22}}}},{"id":"b_2103","type":"button","specials":{"text":"Subscribe"},"events":[{"action":"open_link","id":"ev_2104","target":"https://grow.dijaedadula.com/newsletter","targetURL":"_self","type":"click"}],"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"background":"rgba(0,122,102,1)","borderRadius":"8px","color":"rgba(255,255,255,1)","fontSize":15,"fontWeight":"700","height":40,"left":650,"textAlign":"center","top":16}},"mobile":{"styles":{"background":"rgba(0,122,102,1)","borderRadius":"8px","color":"rgba(255,255,255,1)","fontSize":14,"fontWeight":"700","height":38,"left":270,"textAlign":"center","top":14,"width":130}}}},{"id":"n_2105","type":"text-block","specials":{"text":"<a href=\"https://grow.dijaedadula.com/newsletter\" style=\"color:rgba(26,26,26,1);text-decoration:none\">Newsletter</a>"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":15,"fontWeight":"500","height":24,"left":532,"lineHeight":"1.4","textAlign":"center","top":26,"width":110}},"mobile":{"config":{"hide":true},"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":14,"height":22,"left":270,"lineHeight":"1.4","textAlign":"center","top":22,"width":130}}}},{"id":"n_2106","type":"text-block","specials":{"text":"<a href=\"https://www.dijaedadula.com/blog\" style=\"color:rgba(26,26,26,1);text-decoration:none\">Blog</a>"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":15,"fontWeight":"500","height":24,"left":414,"lineHeight":"1.4","textAlign":"center","top":26,"width":110}},"mobile":{"config":{"hide":true},"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":14,"height":22,"left":270,"lineHeight":"1.4","textAlign":"center","top":22,"width":130}}}},{"id":"l_2107","type":"line","responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"borderColor":"rgba(230,232,236,1)","left":0,"top":63,"width":960}},"mobile":{"styles":{"borderColor":"rgba(230,232,236,1)","left":0,"top":60,"width":420}}}}]},{"id":"sec_2108","type":"section","responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"background":"rgba(255,255,255,1)","height":9917}},"mobile":{"styles":{"background":"rgba(255,255,255,1)","height":13294}}},"children":[{"id":"t_2109","type":"text-block","specials":{"text":"<a href=\"https://www.dijaedadula.com/blog\" style=\"color:rgba(0,122,102,1);text-decoration:underline\">Blog</a> · CRM and automation that sells"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(120,120,120,1)","fontSize":14,"fontWeight":"400","height":25,"left":160,"lineHeight":"1.5","textAlign":"left","top":64,"width":640}},"mobile":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(120,120,120,1)","fontSize":13,"fontWeight":"400","height":24,"left":20,"lineHeight":"1.5","textAlign":"left","top":48,"width":380}}}},{"id":"t_2110","type":"text-block","specials":{"tag":"h1","text":"Appointment No-Shows: Reminder Systems That Actually Work"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":42,"fontWeight":"800","height":154,"left":160,"lineHeight":"1.18","textAlign":"left","top":105,"width":640}},"mobile":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(26,26,26,1)","fontSize":30,"fontWeight":"800","height":112,"left":20,"lineHeight":"1.18","textAlign":"left","top":84,"width":380}}}},{"id":"t_2111","type":"text-block","specials":{"text":"What the research says about reminders, a schedule you can copy, the right channel for each message, ready-to-use templates and the numbers that prove no-shows are going down."},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(85,85,85,1)","fontSize":21,"fontWeight":"400","height":130,"left":160,"lineHeight":"1.5","textAlign":"left","top":277,"width":640}},"mobile":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(85,85,85,1)","fontSize":18,"fontWeight":"400","height":139,"left":20,"lineHeight":"1.5","textAlign":"left","top":210,"width":380}}}},{"id":"t_2112","type":"text-block","specials":{"text":"By <a href=\"https://www.dijaedadula.com/home\" style=\"color:rgba(0,122,102,1);text-decoration:underline\">Dijae Dadula</a> · November 21, 2026 · 10 min read"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(120,120,120,1)","fontSize":14,"fontWeight":"400","height":25,"left":160,"lineHeight":"1.5","textAlign":"left","top":425,"width":640}},"mobile":{"styles":{"color":"rgba(120,120,120,1)","fontSize":13,"fontWeight":"400","height":24,"left":20,"lineHeight":"1.5","textAlign":"left","top":363,"width":380}}}},{"id":"eb_2120","type":"editor-blog","specials":{"html":"<div class='dd-article'>\n<figure class='dd-fig'><div class='dd-shot' style='aspect-ratio:16/9'><img src='https://statics.pancake.vn/web-media-264/08/13/a6/c6/3bdd3d58d375293baab1d82d086dc1747f54bc6af0b5141a51f6dd2f.jpg' alt='Dark flat illustration of a calendar with checked days and one highlighted green slot, a chat bubble with confirm and reschedule buttons, a ringing bell and a clock' width='1536' height='864' loading='lazy'></div></figure>\n<p>A reminder system that works sends more than one reminder, sends the first one a few days ahead, and makes it as easy to reschedule as to confirm. Research on appointment reminders keeps finding the same thing: reminders raise attendance, and two well-timed reminders beat one sent the day before.</p>\n<p>This guide is for clinics, salons, spas, consultants, coaches and any business that lives on a calendar. You will get the research in plain English, a reminder schedule you can copy, the right channel for each message, ready-to-use templates, and the numbers that tell you whether no-shows are actually going down.</p>\n<div class='dd-tldr'>\n<p class='dd-tldr-title'>What you will get</p>\n<ul>\n<li>What the <b>research on reminders</b> shows, with the numbers and the sources.</li>\n<li>A <b>reminder schedule</b> you can copy, from booking to the day of the visit.</li>\n<li>Which <b>channel</b> to use for each message, including what changed on Messenger and WhatsApp in 2026.</li>\n<li><b>Copy-ready templates</b> for confirmation, reminders and the no-reply follow-up.</li>\n<li>A <b>launch checklist</b> and the metrics that prove it works.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n<nav class='dd-toc' aria-label='Table of contents'>\n<p class='dd-toc-title'>In this guide</p>\n<ol>\n<li><a href='#why'>Why do customers miss appointments?</a></li>\n<li><a href='#research'>What does the research say?</a></li>\n<li><a href='#schedule'>When should reminders go out?</a></li>\n<li><a href='#channel'>Which channel should you use?</a></li>\n<li><a href='#templates'>What should a reminder say?</a></li>\n<li><a href='#cancel'>How do you handle cancellations?</a></li>\n<li><a href='#measure'>How do you know it is working?</a></li>\n</ol>\n</nav>\n<h2 id='why'>1. Why do customers miss appointments?</h2>\n<p>Most no-shows are not customers who decided to waste your time. They forgot, their plans changed and cancelling felt like more effort than not showing up, or the details were unclear and they never checked again. The longer the gap between booking and the visit, the more room there is for all three.</p>\n<p>That is why a reminder system has three jobs, not one:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Remind:</b> put the date, time and place in front of the customer again, close enough to the visit to matter.</li>\n<li><b>Confirm:</b> ask for a reply, so you know who is coming and who is silent.</li>\n<li><b>Release:</b> make rescheduling or cancelling a one-tap action, so a slot that will be empty comes back to you early enough to fill it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A reminder that only does the first job helps. A system that does all three turns a surprise gap in your day into a slot you can offer to someone else.</p>\n<h2 id='research'>2. What does the research say about reminders?</h2>\n<p>Reminders are one of the best-studied tools in scheduling, mostly from healthcare, where missed visits are tracked carefully. Three sources are worth knowing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cochrane review, 2013.</b> Across 7 randomized trials with 5,841 participants, attendance was 78.6% with text message reminders against 67.8% with no reminder. Phone call reminders reached 80.3%, and texts and calls performed about the same. In two of the studies, the cost per attended appointment was 55% and 65% lower with texts than with calls.</li>\n<li><b>BMJ Open meta-analysis, 2016.</b> Pooling 21 studies, attendance was 67% with digital notifications against 54% without, and no-shows were 15% against 21%. Sending multiple notifications improved attendance more than sending a single one.</li>\n<li><b>Randomized trial at Kaiser Permanente Colorado, 2018.</b> 54,066 patients across 25 primary care clinics got one reminder 3 days before, one reminder 1 day before, or both. Missed visits were 5.8%, 5.3% and 4.4%. Two reminders beat one, and satisfaction did not drop.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class='dd-fig'>\n<svg class='dk' viewBox='0 0 640 268' role='img' aria-labelledby='fn1t fn1d' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'>\n<title id='fn1t'>Missed visits by reminder schedule</title>\n<desc id='fn1d'>Horizontal bar chart from a 2018 randomized trial of 54,066 patients at Kaiser Permanente Colorado. One reminder 3 days before: 5.8 percent missed. One reminder 1 day before: 5.3 percent missed. Both reminders: 4.4 percent missed, the lowest.</desc>\n<defs><linearGradient id='fn1g' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#1f2224'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#0a0b0c'/></linearGradient></defs>\n<rect x='0' y='0' width='640' height='268' rx='14' fill='url(#fn1g)'/>\n<text x='24' y='34' class='svg-k'>MISSED VISITS BY REMINDER SCHEDULE</text>\n<line x1='200' y1='56' x2='200' y2='218' stroke='#2a2e31' stroke-width='1'/>\n<line x1='320' y1='56' x2='320' y2='218' stroke='#2a2e31' stroke-width='1'/>\n<line x1='440' y1='56' x2='440' y2='218' stroke='#2a2e31' stroke-width='1'/>\n<line x1='560' y1='56' x2='560' y2='218' stroke='#2a2e31' stroke-width='1'/>\n<text x='24' y='88' class='svg-t'>3 days before</text>\n<rect x='200' y='68' width='348' height='30' rx='6' fill='#3a3f43'/>\n<text x='556' y='89' class='svg-t'>5.8%</text>\n<text x='24' y='138' class='svg-t'>1 day before</text>\n<rect x='200' y='118' width='318' height='30' rx='6' fill='#3a3f43'/>\n<text x='526' y='139' class='svg-t'>5.3%</text>\n<text x='24' y='188' class='svg-t'>Both reminders</text>\n<rect x='200' y='168' width='264' height='30' rx='6' fill='#10302a' stroke='#3fbfa6' stroke-width='2'/>\n<text x='472' y='189' class='svg-k'>4.4%</text>\n<text x='200' y='238' class='svg-s'>0%</text>\n<text x='308' y='238' class='svg-s'>2%</text>\n<text x='428' y='238' class='svg-s'>4%</text>\n<text x='548' y='238' class='svg-s'>6%</text>\n<text x='24' y='258' class='svg-s'>54,066 patients, 25 clinics (AJMC, 2018)</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> In a 2018 randomized trial, two reminders (3 days and 1 day before) cut missed visits more than either single reminder.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<p>These studies come from clinics, but the mechanism is general: people forget, and a timely nudge with an easy way to respond changes behavior. A small 2026 study at a single outpatient clinic in Texas points the same way: moving reminder calls from the day before to three or more days ahead, across 653 visits, lowered the no-show rate from 29% to 21%. Treat a single-clinic result as a signal, not a guarantee.</p>\n<div class='dd-note'><span class='dd-note-label'>Dijae's rule</span><p>The first reminder is for remembering. The second is for deciding. Send both, and make “I can't come” as easy to say as “I'll be there.”</p></div>\n<h2 id='schedule'>3. When should reminders go out?</h2>\n<p>Start with a schedule that matches the evidence, then adjust it with your own data:</p>\n<figure class='dd-fig'>\n<svg class='dk' viewBox='0 0 640 250' role='img' aria-labelledby='fn2t fn2d' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'>\n<title id='fn2t'>A reminder schedule from booking to visit</title>\n<desc id='fn2d'>Five steps left to right: at booking send a confirmation with a calendar link, 3 days before send a reminder asking to confirm or reschedule, 1 day before send a short reminder, if there is no reply a person calls, on the day the visit happens. A note says any reschedule releases the slot to the waitlist.</desc>\n<defs><linearGradient id='fn2g' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#1f2224'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#0a0b0c'/></linearGradient>\n<marker id='fn2a' viewBox='0 0 10 10' refX='9' refY='5' markerWidth='7' markerHeight='7' orient='auto'><path d='M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z' fill='#8a8f94'/></marker></defs>\n<rect x='0' y='0' width='640' height='250' rx='14' fill='url(#fn2g)'/>\n<text x='24' y='34' class='svg-k'>BOOKING TO VISIT</text>\n<rect x='24' y='58' width='108' height='86' rx='10' fill='#17191b' stroke='#3a3f43' stroke-width='2'/><text x='36' y='84' class='svg-t'>Booking</text><text x='36' y='106' class='svg-s'>Confirm +</text><text x='36' y='124' class='svg-s'>calendar link</text>\n<rect x='146' y='58' width='108' height='86' rx='10' fill='#10302a' stroke='#3fbfa6' stroke-width='2'/><text x='158' y='84' class='svg-t'>3 days</text><text x='158' y='106' class='svg-s'>Confirm or</text><text x='158' y='124' class='svg-s'>reschedule</text>\n<rect x='268' y='58' width='108' height='86' rx='10' fill='#17191b' stroke='#3a3f43' stroke-width='2'/><text x='280' y='84' class='svg-t'>1 day</text><text x='280' y='106' class='svg-s'>Short</text><text x='280' y='124' class='svg-s'>reminder</text>\n<rect x='390' y='58' width='108' height='86' rx='10' fill='#17191b' stroke='#3a3f43' stroke-width='2'/><text x='402' y='84' class='svg-t'>No reply?</text><text x='402' y='106' class='svg-s'>A person</text><text x='402' y='124' class='svg-s'>calls</text>\n<rect x='512' y='58' width='104' height='86' rx='10' fill='#17191b' stroke='#3a3f43' stroke-width='2'/><text x='524' y='84' class='svg-t'>Visit</text><text x='524' y='106' class='svg-s'>Arrival</text><text x='524' y='124' class='svg-s'>details</text>\n<line x1='132' y1='101' x2='142' y2='101' stroke='#8a8f94' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#fn2a)'/>\n<line x1='254' y1='101' x2='264' y2='101' stroke='#8a8f94' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#fn2a)'/>\n<line x1='376' y1='101' x2='386' y2='101' stroke='#8a8f94' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#fn2a)'/>\n<line x1='498' y1='101' x2='508' y2='101' stroke='#8a8f94' stroke-width='1.5' marker-end='url(#fn2a)'/>\n<path d='M200 144 C200 186 230 196 270 196' stroke='#3fbfa6' stroke-width='2.5' fill='none' stroke-dasharray='8 7' stroke-linecap='round'/>\n<text x='280' y='201' class='svg-t'>Reschedule = slot goes to the waitlist</text>\n<text x='24' y='236' class='svg-s'>Adjust timing to your booking lead time and your own no-show data</text>\n</svg>\n<figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> A copyable reminder schedule. The 3-day message asks for a decision; anyone who stays silent gets a call.</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<ol>\n<li><b>At booking:</b> confirm the date, time, place, what to bring and how to reschedule. Add a calendar link so the appointment lives in the customer's own calendar.</li>\n<li><b>About 3 days before:</b> ask for a reply: confirm or reschedule. This is the message that frees slots early enough to refill.</li>\n<li><b>1 day before:</b> a short reminder with the time and address or video link. No new questions.</li>\n<li><b>No reply after the 3-day message:</b> a person calls or messages directly. Silent customers are the ones most likely to miss the visit.</li>\n<li><b>Same-day appointments booked less than 3 days out:</b> skip straight to a confirmation and one reminder a few hours before.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Keep it to these messages. More is not better once the customer has confirmed; every extra message is a chance for them to mute you.</p>\n<h2 id='channel'>4. Which channel should you use for reminders?</h2>\n<p>The best channel is the one the customer reads and that your tools are allowed to send to at the right time. Rules changed on two Meta channels in 2026, and they matter for reminders:</p>\n<div class='dd-table-wrap'><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>SMS</td><td>The 3-day and 1-day reminders; high reach, no app needed.</td><td>Get consent to text at booking. Rules and costs vary by country.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Email</td><td>The booking confirmation with full details and a calendar file.</td><td>Easy to miss for a next-day reminder; use it alongside a message channel.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>WhatsApp</td><td>Two-way confirm or reschedule in markets where customers live on WhatsApp.</td><td>Reminders outside the 24-hour customer service window need an approved utility template. Since October 1, 2026, utility messages are charged per delivered message even inside an open window.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Messenger</td><td>Booking conversations that start on your Facebook Page.</td><td>Message tags such as CONFIRMED_EVENT_UPDATE stopped working on April 27, 2026. Do not rely on Messenger alone for a reminder days later; collect a phone number or email at booking.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phone call</td><td>Customers who did not reply, and high-value appointments.</td><td>Takes staff time; keep it for the people who stay silent.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For reference, check the Messenger changelog <a href='https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/messenger-platform/changelog' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>here</a> and the WhatsApp pricing page <a href='https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/whatsapp/pricing' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>here</a>. For a full comparison of windows, consent and cost by channel, see <a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/follow-up-channels'>Email, Messenger, SMS or WhatsApp</a>. If you collect phone numbers in Messenger in the Philippines, the <a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/data-privacy-act-chatbots-philippines'>Data Privacy Act guide for chatbots</a> covers consent.</p>\n<h2 id='templates'>5. What should a reminder say?</h2>\n<p>A reminder is read in two seconds on a lock screen. Put the time first, name the business, and give one clear action. Copy these and replace the brackets:</p>\n<div class='dd-code'><button class='dd-copy' type='button'>Copy</button><pre><code>BOOKING CONFIRMATION\nHi [Name], you're booked with [Business] on [Day, Date] at [Time].\nWhere: [Address or video link]\nBring: [What to bring, if anything]\nNeed to change it? Reply RESCHEDULE or use [link].\nAdd to your calendar: [calendar link]\n\n3 DAYS BEFORE\nHi [Name], a reminder from [Business]: [Day] at [Time].\nReply 1 to confirm or 2 to reschedule.\n\n1 DAY BEFORE\nSee you tomorrow at [Time], [Name]. [Address or link]. Running late? Reply here.\n\nNO REPLY (sent by a person)\nHi [Name], this is [Staff name] from [Business]. Just checking you can still make [Day] at [Time]. If not, no problem, I can move it for you.</code></pre></div>\n<p>Three details make the difference. The reply options are numbers, so they work on any phone. Rescheduling is offered in the same breath as confirming, so nobody has to feel awkward. And the no-reply message comes from a named person, because silence after an automated message usually means the customer needs a human.</p>\n<div class='dd-warn'><p>Do not put sensitive details in a reminder. A clinic reminder should say the time and the place, not the treatment. Anyone can read a lock screen.</p></div>\n<h2 id='cancel'>6. How do you handle cancellations and repeat no-shows?</h2>\n<p>A cancellation 3 days out is a win: it is a slot you can still sell. Build the system so every release goes somewhere useful:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Waitlist:</b> when someone reschedules, offer the freed slot to the next person on a waitlist automatically, first to reply gets it.</li>\n<li><b>Reschedule, don't just cancel:</b> offer two or three new slots in the same message, so a “can't make it” becomes a moved booking instead of a lost customer.</li>\n<li><b>Repeat no-shows:</b> tag customers who miss without notice. A person reaches out before their next booking; your policy decides what happens on the second or third time.</li>\n<li><b>Deposits and fees:</b> some businesses take a deposit or charge a late-cancellation fee. That is a policy choice, not an automation. If you use one, state it plainly at booking and in the confirmation, never for the first time in a reminder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If calls are a big part of your bookings, an <a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/ai-voice-receptionist-appointments'>AI voice receptionist</a> can take reschedule requests by phone too. For a full clinic flow from inquiry to review, see <a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/ai-automation-clinics-med-spas'>AI automation for clinics and med spas</a>.</p>\n<h2 id='measure'>7. How do you know the reminder system is working?</h2>\n<p>Measure your no-show rate for at least four weeks before you switch reminders on, so you have a baseline. Then track these every week:</p>\n<div class='dd-table-wrap'><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>How to calculate</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>No-show rate</td><td>Missed without notice ÷ booked appointments</td><td>The headline number. Compare it with your baseline.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Confirmation rate</td><td>Replies of “confirm” ÷ 3-day reminders sent</td><td>Whether customers read and act on the reminder.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Early release rate</td><td>Reschedules or cancels 24+ hours ahead ÷ bookings</td><td>Whether gaps reach you in time to refill.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Refill rate</td><td>Released slots rebooked ÷ released slots</td><td>Whether the waitlist is doing its job.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Delivery failures</td><td>Failed or bounced messages ÷ sent</td><td>Bad numbers, missing consent or channel problems.</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Before launch, run this test with five real bookings on staff phones:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each message arrives at the right time, in the customer's time zone.</li>\n<li>Replying 1 marks the booking as confirmed in your calendar or CRM.</li>\n<li>Replying 2 offers new slots and moves the booking without a person.</li>\n<li>A booking with no reply creates a call task for a named staff member.</li>\n<li>A cancelled booking stops all remaining reminders and alerts the waitlist.</li>\n<li>No reminder shows treatment or other sensitive details.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Accept the system when all six pass. Review the numbers weekly for the first month, then monthly.</p>\n<p>An honest limitation: reminders reduce no-shows, they do not remove them. Most of the research comes from healthcare, results vary by business and customer group, and some missed visits come from causes a message cannot fix, such as transport, cost or a booking made too far ahead. Use your own baseline, not someone else's numbers, to judge the result.</p>\n<h2 id='faq'>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How many appointment reminders should I send?</h3>\n<p>Two before the visit is a strong default: one about 3 days before that asks the customer to confirm or reschedule, and a short one the day before. A 2018 randomized trial found two reminders reduced missed visits more than either one alone. Add a booking confirmation and a call for anyone who does not reply.</p>\n<h3>Are text reminders as good as phone calls?</h3>\n<p>For most appointments, yes. A 2013 Cochrane review found text and phone reminders performed about the same for attendance, and texts cost less per attended appointment in the studies that measured cost. Keep calls for customers who do not reply.</p>\n<h3>Can I send appointment reminders through Facebook Messenger?</h3>\n<p>Only within limits. Meta retired the CONFIRMED_EVENT_UPDATE message tag and two others on April 27, 2026, so you cannot rely on Messenger for a reminder several days after the last message. Collect a phone number or email at booking and send reminders there.</p>\n<h3>What is a good no-show rate?</h3>\n<p>It depends on your industry, customers and how far ahead people book, so there is no universal target. Measure your own rate for four weeks before launch and judge the reminder system by how far it moves that number.</p>\n<div class='dd-cta'>\n<p class='dd-cta-title'>Fill the gaps in your calendar</p>\n<p>Want a reminder, confirmation and waitlist system built around your booking tool and channels? <a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/book'>Book a free AI Automation Blueprint call</a> and we will map your schedule, messages and metrics.</p>\n<p>Prefer to learn it step by step? <a href='https://grow.dijaedadula.com/newsletter'>Join the free weekly newsletter</a>.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>References</h2>\n<ol class='dd-refs'>\n<li><a href='https://www.cochrane.org/evidence/CD007458_mobile-phone-messaging-reminders-attendance-healthcare-appointments' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Cochrane: Mobile phone messaging reminders for attendance at healthcare appointments</a>, review published December 5, 2013 (8 trials, 6,615 participants).</li>\n<li><a href='https://bmjopen.bmj.com/content/6/10/e012116.full' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Using digital notifications to improve attendance in clinic: systematic review and meta-analysis</a>. BMJ Open, 2016;6(10):e012116.</li>\n<li><a href='https://www.ajmc.com/view/optimizing-number-and-timing-of-appointment-reminders-a-randomized-trial' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Optimizing Number and Timing of Appointment Reminders: A Randomized Trial</a>. American Journal of Managed Care, 2018;24(8):377-384.</li>\n<li><a href='https://uta.edu/news/news-releases/2026/03/18/good-call-earlier-reminders-cut-missed-doctor-visits' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>University of Texas at Arlington: Good call: Earlier reminders cut missed doctor visits</a>, March 18, 2026 (study in the Journal of Doctoral Nursing Practice).</li>\n<li><a href='https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/messenger-platform/changelog' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Meta: Messenger Platform changelog</a>, entry of March 27, 2026 (message tags deprecated from April 27, 2026). Accessed November 21, 2026.</li>\n<li><a href='https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/business-messaging/whatsapp/pricing' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Meta: Pricing on the WhatsApp Business Platform</a> (utility messages charged in open windows from October 1, 2026). Accessed November 21, 2026.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Keep reading</h2>\n<ul class='dd-more'>\n<li><a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/ai-voice-receptionist-appointments'>AI Voice Receptionist for Appointment Businesses: How It Works and What to Check</a></li>\n<li><a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/ai-automation-clinics-med-spas'>AI Automation for Clinics and Med Spas: Bookings, Reminders and Reviews</a></li>\n<li><a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/follow-up-channels'>Email, Messenger, SMS or WhatsApp: Choosing the Right Channel for Automated Follow-Up</a></li>\n<li><a href='https://www.dijaedadula.com/blog'>See all posts on the blog</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n"},"responsive":{"desktop":{"styles":{"height":9378,"left":160,"top":482,"width":640}},"mobile":{"styles":{"height":12830,"left":20,"top":415,"width":380}}}},{"id":"h_css2121","type":"html-box","specials":{"html":"&lt;style&gt;.dd-article ul{list-style:disc outside !important}.dd-article ol{list-style:dec